@page "/Projects/LoadingScreen"
<div class="ProjectPage">
  <DefaultProjectHeading CurrentProjectURL="Projects/LoadingScreen" />
  <div class="ProjectDescription">
    <div>
      <h3>Project description</h3>
      <span>
        My second and way smaller project at Birungi Studio was a loading screen. The one that was used before we
        implemented mine was one on an artificial timer. That meant it just always waited 3 seconds before continuing.
        It was implemented this way because there was nothing to load yet. Now with my reworked chunk system there is
        something to wait on however.
      </span>
    </div>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@loadingScreenInterface" />
    <span>
      I decided that even though the system was currently only for the chunk system, it should probably still be generic
      since more systems might require loading in the future. So I decided on a sequential loading method. It first
      loads the scene. When the scene has loaded it looks for scripts implementing the loading interface.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@loadingScreenSceneLoad" />
    <span>
      Then the script collects those and waits for them.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@loadingScreenYieldAll" />
    <span>
      When its done loading it fires an event so other scripts that are dependent on the loaded scripts can react and
      execute their own logic.
    </span>
    <CodeBlock CodeString="@loadingScreenEvent" />
    <span>
      And thus a short but effective loading screen with an aggregating pattern was born.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

@code
{
  string loadingScreenInterface = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["loadingScreenInterface"];
  string loadingScreenSceneLoad = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["loadingScreenSceneLoad"];
  string loadingScreenYieldAll = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["loadingScreenYieldAll"];
  string loadingScreenEvent = CodeBlockDictionary.CodeBlocks["loadingScreenEvent"];
}